<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nusantara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nusantara is the planned new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, under construction since 2022 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. The project, announced by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joko-widodo">Joko Widodo</a>, aims to relieve crowded and sinking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> and to spread development beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Its name is an old Javanese term for the islands of the archipelago.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nusantara.jpg" alt="Nusantara" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PUPR Permukiman Kaltim (East Kalimantan Public Works and Public Housing Office)as watermarked in the video · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nusantara,_June_2024.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nusantara lies on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a> regencies of Penajam North Paser and Kutai Kartanegara, next to the port city of Balikpapan. Its coast stretches east to the Makassar Strait and south to Balikpapan Bay. The landscape is hilly and was previously an industrial forest. The designated area covers 2,560 square kilometres, of which 68.56 square kilometres form the Main Governmental Zone. The climate is tropical rainforest, with no real dry season and about 3,229 millimetres of rain a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nūsāntara is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> term meaning archipelago or islands, formed from the native nūsa, island, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> loanword, antarā. The earliest evidence dates from the 1200s, in the Mula Malurung inscription from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>, the predecessor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. It was chosen as the city's name to express the national vision called Wawasan Nusantara and the country's status as an archipelagic state.</p><p>A move of the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a> had been proposed since the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, who in 1957 inaugurated Palangka Raya with the idea that it might become the capital. In April 2017 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joko-widodo">Joko Widodo</a> ordered the national planning ministry to assess sites outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, as part of a strategy to reduce inequality between Java and the other islands. The minister of national development planning announced the name Nusantara on 17 January 2022, and construction began that year, delayed until after the vaccination campaign against COVID-19. The plan has five phases, lasting until the hundredth anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a>.</p><p>Soil and water from historically significant sites in all 34 provinces were brought to the new capital, among them water and soil from Kutai Lama in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a> and from former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nusantara is administered not by an elected council but by the Nusantara Capital City Authority, an agency directly accountable to the central government, whose head is appointed by the president. It is planned as a national capital region at the provincial level, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, which suffers from overpopulation and sinking land. The design aims at sustainability, with most journeys by public transport, cycling or walking, renewable energy and a tenth of the area given to food production.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nusantara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Today Indonesia is a presidential republic of thirty-eight provinces with regular, competitive elections, and since 2022 has been building a new capital, <b>Nusantara</b>, in East Kalimantan on Borneo, while Jakarta sinks and suffers chronic congestion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…is sung throughout the archipelago and belongs to its peoples, Malaysians and Indonesians alike: Tourism Minister Adnan Mansor called it a folk song of the <b>Nusantara</b>, of which Malaysia is part, and Rais Yatim, Minister of Culture, Arts and Heritage, recognised it as shared between Indonesia and Malaysia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rasa-sayang">Rasa Sayang</a></li></ul></section>
